* stcolor.scheme
* dash translation of Stata's stcolor scheme.
*
* PROVENANCE
*   Source Stata scheme : /Applications/StataNow/ado/base/s/scheme-stcolor.scheme
*                         (#include chain: s2color -> stcolor)
*   Style files         : /Applications/StataNow/ado/base/style/*.style
*   Stata version       : StataNow 19.5 (MP), born 12 Aug 2026
*   Extracted           : 2026-09-19 (fixture generated_utc 2026-09-19T16:13:55Z)
*   Derived from        : test/fixtures/scheme_truth.json, entry "stcolor",
*                         generated by scripts/extract_scheme_truth.py
*   Last known true as of the extraction date above.  Re-run the extractor and
*   re-diff this file after any Stata upgrade.
*
* WHY EVERY COLOUR IS WRITTEN AS RESOLVED #rrggbb HEX
*   Stata's stcolor palette uses the stc1..stc15 colour styles, which are NOT in
*   OptionTranslator._COLOR_MAP.  SchemeParser._color() swallows an unresolvable
*   token and returns its "#000000" default, so a name-based translation of this
*   scheme renders every series black.  Hex is self-contained and immune to that.
*
* SELF-CONTAINED BY DESIGN
*   Stata's scheme-stcolor.scheme #includes scheme-s2color.scheme.  This file
*   deliberately does NOT #include dash's schemes/s2color.scheme: that file is an
*   approximation written in Stata colour NAMES and would contribute nothing here
*   but a risk of name-resolution fallback.  Every value below is stated outright.
*
* Property categories used by dash SchemeParser:
*   color, symbol, linepattern, linewidth, intensity, gsize, graphsize,
*   symbolsize, clockdir, numstyle

label "Stata color"

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Background and region colors
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
color background      #ffffff
color plotregion      #ffffff
color legend          #ffffff
color legend_line     #ffffff
color major_grid      #f0f0f0
color minor_grid      #505050
color heading         #000000
color axisline        #000000

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Per-plot color cycle (p1-p15) -- Stata colour styles stc1..stc15, resolved.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
color p1       #1a85ff
color p2       #d41159
color p3       #00bf7f
color p4       #ffd400
color p5       #4f2c99
color p6       #ff6333
color p7       #4db7ff
color p8       #7c0015
color p9       #0fefaf
color p10      #faa307
color p11      #758bfd
color p12      #fed9b7
color p13      #08234c
color p14      #f88dad
color p15      #0f5156

* Per-plot line color (for line/connected plots).
* Stata's stcolor declares no 'color p#line'; these resolve through the
* p#stub -> pstub -> p# -> p fallback chain onto 'color p#'.  Written out
* explicitly so dash reads the same value without relying on a fallback.
color p1line      #1a85ff
color p2line      #d41159
color p3line      #00bf7f
color p4line      #ffd400
color p5line      #4f2c99
color p6line      #ff6333
color p7line      #4db7ff
color p8line      #7c0015
color p9line      #0fefaf
color p10line     #faa307
color p11line     #758bfd
color p12line     #fed9b7
color p13line     #08234c
color p14line     #f88dad
color p15line     #0f5156

* Per-plot bar/area fill color (resolved through the same fallback onto p#).
color p1bar       #1a85ff
color p2bar       #d41159
color p3bar       #00bf7f
color p4bar       #ffd400
color p5bar       #4f2c99
color p6bar       #ff6333
color p7bar       #4db7ff
color p8bar       #7c0015
color p9bar       #0fefaf
color p10bar      #faa307
color p11bar      #758bfd
color p12bar      #fed9b7
color p13bar      #08234c
color p14bar      #f88dad
color p15bar      #0f5156

* Per-plot marker fill color (resolved through the same fallback onto p#).
color p1mark      #1a85ff
color p2mark      #d41159
color p3mark      #00bf7f
color p4mark      #ffd400
color p5mark      #4f2c99
color p6mark      #ff6333
color p7mark      #4db7ff
color p8mark      #7c0015
color p9mark      #0fefaf
color p10mark     #faa307
color p11mark     #758bfd
color p12mark     #fed9b7
color p13mark     #08234c
color p14mark     #f88dad
color p15mark     #0f5156

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Marker symbol cycle.
* Stata's stcolor sets a single class default, 'symbol p circle', so every
* plot in the cycle is a circle.  dash's SchemeParser reads 'symbol p#' with
* no class fallback, so the one declared value is expanded to all 15.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
symbol p1      circle
symbol p2      circle
symbol p3      circle
symbol p4      circle
symbol p5      circle
symbol p6      circle
symbol p7      circle
symbol p8      circle
symbol p9      circle
symbol p10     circle
symbol p11     circle
symbol p12     circle
symbol p13     circle
symbol p14     circle
symbol p15     circle

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Line patterns.
* Same situation: Stata declares only 'linepattern p solid'.  Expanded to the
* 15 'linepattern p#line' keys dash actually reads.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
linepattern p1line    solid
linepattern p2line    solid
linepattern p3line    solid
linepattern p4line    solid
linepattern p5line    solid
linepattern p6line    solid
linepattern p7line    solid
linepattern p8line    solid
linepattern p9line    solid
linepattern p10line   solid
linepattern p11line   solid
linepattern p12line   solid
linepattern p13line   solid
linepattern p14line   solid
linepattern p15line   solid

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Line widths (declared Stata style names; 'medium' = .30 Stata linewidth units)
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
linewidth p           medium
linewidth major_grid  medium

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Fill intensities.
* 'intensity area' is not set by any Stata base scheme, including stcolor; it
* resolves through the bare 'intensity' class default, which is 'full' here.
* The resolved value is recorded, not guessed.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
intensity p        inten80
intensity bar      full
intensity area     full

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Font sizes (Stata gsize keywords, as declared)
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
gsize text          medium
gsize heading       large
gsize axis_title    medsmall
gsize tick_label    medsmall
gsize note          small

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Graph dimensions, in inches, as Stata declares them.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
graphsize x         7.5
graphsize y         4.5

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Default marker size for the p cycle, and legend placement.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
symbolsize p           small
clockdir legend_position  3
numstyle legend_cols      1

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* HONEST GAPS -- properties in the fixture that this file deliberately omits
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* The fixture records two more resolved properties for stcolor:
*   draw_major_hgrid = yes   (Stata directive: yesno draw_major_hgrid)
*   draw_major_vgrid = no    (Stata directive: yesno draw_major_grid)
* SchemeParser._init_properties() reads no 'yesno' key, so writing them would
* add vocabulary dash does not consume.  They are named here rather than
* silently dropped.  Nothing in the fixture's stcolor entry is null, and its
* "unresolvable" list is empty, so no value in this file is a guess.

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Grid visibility (Stata `yesno` directives)
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* GRID VISIBILITY.  A Stata grid belongs to an AXIS and the key is named for
* that axis's orientation, not for the direction of the lines:
*   style/axisstyle-horizontal_default.style:18  draw_major_grid <- draw_major_hgrid
*   style/axisstyle-vertical_default.style:18    draw_major_grid <- draw_major_vgrid
* The horizontal axis is x, and its major grid lines are the VERTICAL ones at
* the x ticks; the vertical axis is y, and its lines are HORIZONTAL.
* `yesno draw_major_grid' below is transcribed faithfully from Stata's file but
* is DEAD at scheme level: grep "scheme draw_major_grid" over
* /Applications/StataNow/ado/base/style/ returns nothing.  dash used to read it
* for the y axis, which is why every scheme reported "no grid" and dash drew
* both anyway.
yesno draw_major_hgrid      yes
yesno draw_major_vgrid yes
yesno draw_major_grid       no

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Added 22sep2026 by scripts/extract_scheme_truth.py (WP-16): directives
* Stata consumes that the translation above did not carry.  Values are
* resolved from the same Stata #include chain and style files as the
* rest of this file (chain: s2color -> stcolor); colours are
* pre-resolved #rrggbb.  Re-running the script re-checks every directive
* in this file and appends only what is missing.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

* Minor grid switches -- EFFECTIVE per-axis values.  twoway builds the x
* axis with `axisstyle horizontal_default' and the y axis with `axisstyle
* vertical_default'; the scheme maps each role to a style name, and that
* style file names the `yesno' key Stata actually reads:
*   x axis: axisstyle horizontal_default = horizontal_default
*           -> style/axisstyle-horizontal_default.style
*           -> draw_major_hgrid / draw_minor_hgrid
*   y axis: axisstyle vertical_default = vertical_default
*           -> style/axisstyle-vertical_default.style
*           -> draw_major_vgrid / draw_minor_vgrid
* The draw_major_hgrid / draw_major_vgrid lines above hold the same
* effective values.  `yesno draw_minor_grid' is transcribed from Stata's
* file but, like draw_major_grid, no style file reads it.
yesno draw_minor_hgrid  no
yesno draw_minor_vgrid  no
yesno draw_minor_grid   no

* Text colours, one per textbox style: style/textboxstyle-<name>.style
* reads `color <name>'.  title() is heading (above), subtitle()
* subheading, caption() body, note() small_body, legend keys key_label,
* axis titles axis_title, tick labels tick_label.  `color text' and
* `color foreground' are the scheme's general text and foreground
* colours.
color subheading  #000000
color body        #000000
color small_body  #000000
color text        #000000
color axis_title  #000000
color tick_label  #000000
color key_label   #000000
color foreground  #000000

* Text sizes, one per textbox style (Stata gsize style names; the
* fixture's _meta.units gives each name's relative value and its points
* at Stata's 4-inch reference).  note() is drawn with small_body: the
* `gsize note' line above is not the key Stata's note textbox reads.
gsize subheading  medium
gsize body        medsmall
gsize small_body  small
gsize key_label   medsmall

* Minor grid and axis line widths (Stata linewidth style names).
linewidth minor_grid  thin
linewidth axisline    thin

* Legend rows; 0 means not fixed, so the column count above decides.
numstyle legend_rows  0

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* HONEST GAP (project rule 2) -- the p#lineplot keys
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Stata does not read a plain `twoway line` plot's line from the
* `p#line` scheme keys.  It reaches the `p#lineplot` keys through two
* style files:
*   style/seriesstyle-p#line.style:5   set line    scheme p#line
*   style/linestyle-p#line.style:4-7   set width   scheme p#lineplot
*                                      set color   scheme p#lineplot
*                                      set pattern scheme p#lineplot
* So `linepattern p#lineplot` is the dash pattern of `twoway line`, while
* the `linepattern p#line` block above is what style/linestyle-p#.style
* reads -- the connecting line of `twoway connected`.  Stata documents the
* three keys in scheme_line_plots.sthlp:29-34 as the colour, thickness and
* pattern "for #th line plot".
*
* NO `color p#lineplot` OR `linepattern p#lineplot` DIRECTIVE IS WRITTEN IN
* THIS FILE, because stcolor's #include chain (s2color -> stcolor) declares
* none at any level. Of all the scheme files Stata ships, only
* scheme-s2mono.scheme and scheme-s2gmanual.scheme contain a `lineplot`
* directive at all; the three occurrences in scheme-s2color.scheme (lines
* 457, 604 and 1250) are commented-out examples, not settings. There is
* therefore nothing to translate here, and a value written in would be a
* fabrication.
*
* Recorded for the reader, NOT as directives, because these are values Stata
* falls back to rather than values stcolor writes
* (scheme_line_plots.sthlp:52-56: "If a scheme does not contain some of the
* line-plot specific entries above, then the look of those elements of line
* plots will be determined by default entries that are shared among all
* plottypes"; the shared entries are `color p#` and `linepattern p`, per
* scheme_shared_plots.sthlp:20-31):
*   linepattern p#lineplot -> linepattern p = solid
*       (/Applications/StataNow/ado/base/s/scheme-s2color.scheme:599)
*   color       p#lineplot -> color p#, i.e. the `color p1`..`color p15`
*       values already written above
*       (/Applications/StataNow/ado/base/s/scheme-stcolor.scheme:47-61)
